00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:49.060
The first credits scene in Thor: Ragnarok
arrives just after the end of the animated

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.809
credits and features Thor and Loki who've
just left Asgard and are now on a spaceship

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:58.320
headed to Earth, though Loki wonders whether
it's really a good idea for him to return

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.860
there given what he did in The Avengers movie.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.789
Thor quips that the God of Thunder is really
popular on Earth and everything will be fine.

00:01:05.789 --> 00:01:10.850
But just as Thor says that, an enormous craft
comes into view, completely dwarfing Thor

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.179
and Loki's spaceship by comparison.

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:18.439
This ominous moment is teasing the arrival
of Thanos in what is probably one of his Sanctuary

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:19.439
ships.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.109
In the comics, Sanctuary is the name given
to several of Thanos's space arks which he's

00:01:23.109 --> 00:01:25.759
used as a base for conducting operations.

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:30.340
As for the MCU, Sanctuary is identified in
Guardians of the Galaxy as Thanos's domain

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.329
in space and it also briefly appeared in The
Avengers.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:38.319
In the Avengers: Infinity War trailer shown
at D23 and Comic Con, there's a moment where

00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:42.560
Thor appears floating through space and then
lands on the windshield of the Guardian's

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.979
Milano ship.

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:47.729
Which means that the Ragnarok mid-credits
scene is probably setting up for Thanos to

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.479
meet Thor and Loki in space.

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:54.079
It looks like Thor is going to get beaten
by Thanos who might even destroy the ship

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:55.079
Thor is on.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:59.569
And it might also explain why, in the Infinity
War trailer, Loki appears to be offering the

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:02.739
Tesseract, aka the Space Stone, to Thanos.

00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:08.069
In Thor Ragnarok, there's a strong hint that
Loki steals the Tesseract for himself meaning

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:10.490
Loki will likely have it on him when they
meet Thanos.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.129
And remember that in The Avengers movie, Loki
had agreed to steal the Tesseract for Thanos

00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:17.570
in return for ruling over Earth.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.860
Thanos may well be doubtless be furious at
Loki for his failure so far, so Loki may need

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.180
to give up the infinity stone to save himself.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.990
The other Ragnarok post-credits scene is a
humorous one that features the Grandmaster

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.469
stumbling out of a container on Sakaar.

00:02:31.469 --> 00:02:35.349
As he staggers out, he meets a group of Sakaarans
staring at him.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:40.129
In Thor: Ragnarok, the Grandmaster is an eccentric
dictator who rules over the planet Sakaar,

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:44.150
where he enslaves gladiators to fight in his
Contest of Champions.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:48.650
The gladiators eventually rebel against the
Grandmaster, and in this post-credits scene,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:53.030
the Grandmaster congratulates the Sakaarans
on a successful revolution and pats himself

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.640
on the back for helping to make the uprising
such a huge success and declares the rebellion

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.840
to be a tie.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.650
Jeff Goldblum delivers some of his great trademark
off-the-wall humour in this scene which also

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.590
leaves the door open for him to return in
a future MCU movie.

